PostgreSQL 19's regression tests in the planner pass: the differences from
PostgreSQL's expected output that come of a statement of theirs being
Cloudberry's SQL too, which the port speaks.  Each file is one test's
difference in the form ../canon.pl gives it, named for the expected output
it differs from.  run.sh compares every other test of PostgreSQL's line for
line in this pass; under ORCA, orca/ holds a test's whole difference, this
one among it.

tablespace  ALTER TABLE testschema.part SET TABLESPACE, of a partitioned
  table, which moves its partitions too, as Cloudberry's ALTER TABLE does
  unless ONLY is given (its ATPrepCmd() recurses AT_SetTableSpace; gp_sql's
  GpPartitionSetTablespace(), partition.c): after the test's second one, to
  pg_default, part_2, part_3 and part_56 are in pg_default where PostgreSQL
  19 leaves them in regress_tblspace.  Cloudberry's own copy of the test
  says ALTER TABLE ONLY there, to keep PostgreSQL's answer
  (src/test/singlenode_regress/input/tablespace.source).

window  count() with no argument and no star, which PostgreSQL refuses
  ("count(*) must be used to call a parameterless aggregate function") and
  Cloudberry's parse analysis takes for count(*), as the port's grammar does
  (gp_desugar.c, count_close()): the test's SELECT count() OVER () FROM tenk1
  answers ten thousand rows, as Cloudberry's own copy of the test answers
  (with a LIMIT 5 it added, src/test/regress/expected/window.out).
